{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>And I pleaded with HaShem at that time, saying.</b> One needs to understand the saying of <b>at that time.</b> And our Sages of blessed memory said (Sifrei Devarim 26): when he saw that Sihon and Og fell before him, etc. According to their words, it refers back to what precedes it, namely the war of Sihon and Og, and accordingly the reason for the verse is to say that apart from what was at that time I would not have prayed, for the decree had already been decreed." "<b>And it appears</b> to say, by way of further noting, the saying of <b>לֵאמֹר</b>, and our Sages of blessed memory expounded (Sifrei) that Moses said to HaShem: answer me regarding my words, whether I am entering the land — and this is the way of derash. However, the meaning of saying \"at that time\" is the time when HaShem swore concerning the wilderness generation that they would not see the land, as He said in the preceding portion, and Moses was included in the oath of the decree, as He said (Deuteronomy 1:37): ״גַּם בִּי הִתְאַנַּף ה׳ בִּגְלַלְכֶם״. At that time he pleaded with HaShem to annul His decree and to release the oath, as alluded to in the verse ״אַתָּה הַחִלּוֹתָ״, and our Sages of blessed memory expounded it in the sense of releasing an oath, as I shall explain nearby. And the reason for the verse is to inform them, lest they suspect him that he prayed only for himself to annul his decree and did not care about his generation but turned a blind eye from them and that entire generation perished. Therefore he said <b>at that time</b> — meaning, before the decree was carried out he pleaded with HaShem." "<b>And furthermore,</b> though at the beginning we do not find that he mentioned anything other than for himself and not for his people, nevertheless his intention was with wisdom, that he prayed for himself to release the oath upon him, and afterward he would say before Him to annul it also for Israel, and the matter would be easy to achieve by the rule of a vow: if part of it is released, all of it is released (Nedarim 66a). And this is what it means by <b>לֵאמֹר</b> — meaning, the purpose of the matter is not in the words spoken but to say yet other things. And this he did with wisdom, for it would be more readily acceptable, his prayer for himself to release the oath of the decree upon him, since he is blameless and righteous, than to release the oath of Israel's decree, they being bearers of sin — and all the words of Moses declare the righteousness HaShem has done." "<b>Furthermore,</b> the intention is to examine four conditions required for the acceptance of prayer: first, that one pray like a poor man knocking at the door, in the manner of the verse (Proverbs 18:23): ״תַּחֲנוּנִים יְדַבֶּר רָשׁ״. Second, that one seek from the Source of mercy. Third, the time of prayer, in the manner of the verse (Psalms 69:14): ״וַאֲנִי תְפִלָּתִי לְךָ ה׳ עֵת רָצוֹן״. Fourth, that one's prayer be explicitly stated and not bear an improper interpretation — as in the account brought in the Midrash (Esther Rabbah 7:24) concerning that gentile and the Jew, etc., and the gentile's animal gave birth and the Jew was forced to carry it on his shoulder, etc., end. And this is what Moses intended in his statement here: <b>וָאֶתְחַנַּן</b> — an expression of supplications, this corresponds to the first condition. <b>אֶל ה׳</b> — that he sought from the Source of mercy, this corresponds to the second condition. <b>בָּעֵת הַהִיא</b> — meaning, at the known time for acceptance of prayer, which is a time of favor, for who knows the time of favor like Moses. And it is possible that this was at the time when HaShem said to him ״הָחֵל רָשׁ״ the land of Sihon and Og, and this accords with the words of our Sages of blessed memory who said: after he conquered the land of Sihon and Og, etc.; this corresponds to the third condition. <b>לֵאמֹר</b> — meaning, he spelled out his words as required, so that his words would not bear an improper interpretation; this corresponds to the fourth condition. And according to the words of our Sages of blessed memory who said \"at that time\" means when he saw the war of Sihon and Og, the reason for <b>לֵאמֹר</b> follows from ״אַתָּה הַחִלּוֹתָ״. And I have already written in several places that when the speaker does not state the utterance in his own language but includes the intended meaning in another's language, he says ״לֵאמֹר״ — meaning, this is the intention of the statement. So too in what is before us, he said ״לֵאמֹר״ to alert you to this. And there are among our Sages of blessed memory who said (Yalkut Shimoni 19): \"at that time\" — when He said to him (Numbers 20:12) ״לֹא תָבִיאוּ אֶת הַקָּהָל״, etc.; and there are those who said (Midrash Rabbah): when He said to him (Numbers 27:18) ״קַח אֶת יְהוֹשֻׁעַ״, etc. And it is possible that at all these times he prayed, and regarding each of those times he says ״בָּעֵת הַהִיא״ — and these and those are the words of the living Elohim."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:14:56.277058380Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}